{"records":[{"id":"bgxfwsg3sb","title":"A comprehensive analysis and resource to use CRISPR-Cas13 for broad-spectrum targeting of RNA viruses. Lin et al.","description":"The COVID-19 pandemic caused by SARS-CoV-2 and variants has led to significant mortality. We recently reported that an RNA-targeting CRISPR-Cas13 system, termed prophylactic antiviral CRISPR in human (PAC-MAN), offered an antiviral strategy against SARS-CoV-2 and influenza A virus. Here, we expand in silico analysis to use PAC-MAN to target a broad spectrum of human- or livestock-infectious RNA viruses with high specificity, coverage, and predicted efficiency. Our analysis reveals that a minimal set of 14 crRNAs is able to target >90% of human-infectious viruses across 10 RNA virus families. We predict that a set of 5 experimentally validated crRNAs can target new SARS-CoV-2 variant sequences with 0 mismatches. We also build an online resource (crispr-pacman.stanford.edu) to support community use of CRISPR-Cas13 for broad-spectrum RNA virus targeting. Our work provides a new bioinformatic resource for using CRISPR-Cas13 to target diverse RNA viruses in order to facilitate development of CRISPR-based antivirals. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-03-22T16:33:31.569Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/bgxfwsg3sb","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/bgxfwsg3sb"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Xueqiu","last_name":"Lin","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yanxia","last_name":"Liu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Augustine","last_name":"Chemparathy","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Tara","last_name":"Pande","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Marie","last_name":"La Russa","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Stanley","last_name":"Qi","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Bioengineering","Antiviral","Computational Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"9x47wzy8x7","title":"A study of \"Genome-wide CRISPR screens reveal host factors critical for SARS-CoV-2 infection\" by Wei et al. Cell 2020","description":"Identification of host genes essential for SARS-CoV-2 infection may reveal novel therapeutic targets and inform our understanding of COVID-19 pathogenesis. Here, we performed genome-wide CRISPR screens in Vero-E6 cells with SARS-CoV-2, MERS-CoV, bat coronavirus HKU5 expressing the SARS-CoV-1 spike, and VSV expressing the SARS-CoV-2 spike. Together this identifies potential therapeutic targets for SARS-CoV-2 and reveals SARS-lineage specific and pan-coronavirus host factors that regulate susceptibility to highly pathogenic coronaviruses.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-10-15T21:29:14.06Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/9x47wzy8x7","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/9x47wzy8x7"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Craig","last_name":"Wilen","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["CRISPR","Middle East Respiratory Syndrome-Related Coronavirus","Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus 2","COVID-19"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"gcdpsxd6d5","title":"RNAi screening using HK-2 cells: insights into the roles of soluble ACE2 in SARS-CoV-2 infection. Yeung et al","description":"Severe acute respiratory syndrome coronavirus 2 (SARS-CoV-2) can cause acute respiratory disease and multiorgan failure. Finding human host factors that are essential for SARS-CoV-2 infection could facilitate the formulation of treatment strategies. Using a human kidney cell line—HK-2—that is highly susceptible to SARS-CoV-2, we performed a genome-wide RNAi screening and identified virus dependency factors (VDFs), which play regulatory roles in biological pathways linked to clinical manifestations of SARS-CoV-2 infection. Importantly, we discovered an overlooked role of a secretory form of SARS-CoV-2 receptor, soluble angiotensin converting enzyme 2 (sACE2), in SARS-CoV-2 infection. Further investigations revealed that SARS-CoV-2 exploits receptor-mediated endocytosis through interaction between its spike with sACE2 or sACE2-vasopressin via AT1 or AVPR1B, respectively. Our identification of new VDFs and the regulatory effect of sACE2 on SARS-CoV-2 infection shed new insights into pathogenesis and cell entry mechanism of SARS-CoV-2 as well as potential treatment strategies for COVID-19.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-02-22T23:41:07.557Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/gcdpsxd6d5","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/gcdpsxd6d5"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Man Lung","last_name":"Yeung","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Screening","RNA Knockdown"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"72tr8jj8y4","title":"RNAi screening using HK-2 cells: insights into the roles of soluble ACE2 in SARS-CoV-2 infection. Yeung et al","description":"Severe acute respiratory syndrome coronavirus 2 (SARS-CoV-2) can cause acute respiratory disease and multiorgan failure. Finding human host factors that are essential for SARS-CoV-2 infection could facilitate the formulation of treatment strategies. Using a human kidney cell line—HK-2—that is highly susceptible to SARS-CoV-2, we performed a genome-wide RNAi screening and identified virus dependency factors (VDFs), which play regulatory roles in biological pathways linked to clinical manifestations of SARS-CoV-2 infection. Importantly, we discovered an overlooked role of a secretory form of SARS-CoV-2 receptor, soluble angiotensin converting enzyme 2 (sACE2), in SARS-CoV-2 infection. Further investigations revealed that SARS-CoV-2 exploits receptor-mediated endocytosis through interaction between its spike with sACE2 or sACE2-vasopressin via AT1 or AVPR1B, respectively. Our identification of new VDFs and the regulatory effect of sACE2 on SARS-CoV-2 infection shed new insights into pathogenesis and cell entry mechanism of SARS-CoV-2 as well as potential treatment strategies for COVID-19.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-02-22T23:48:03.555Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/72tr8jj8y4","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/72tr8jj8y4"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Man Lung","last_name":"Yeung","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Vasopressin","Screening","RNA Knockdown","Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus 2","COVID-19"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"9rc2n4bkmv","title":"Functional interrogation of a SARS-CoV-2 host protein interactome identifies unique and shared coronavirus host factors. Hoffmann et al.","description":"The ongoing SARS-CoV-2 pandemic has devastated the global economy and claimed more than one million lives, presenting an urgent global health crisis. To identify host factors required for infection by SARS-CoV-2 and seasonal coronaviruses, we designed a focused high-coverage CRISPR-Cas9 library targeting 332 members of a recently published SARS-CoV-2 protein interactome. We leveraged the compact nature of this library to systematically screen SARS-CoV-2 at two physiologically relevant temperatures (33 ºC and 37 ºC) along with three related coronaviruses (HCoV-229E, HCoV-NL63, and HCoV-OC43), allowing us to probe this interactome at a much higher resolution relative to genome scale studies. This approach yielded several new insights, including unexpected virus-specific differences in Rab GTPase requirements and GPI anchor biosynthesis, as well as identification of multiple pan-coronavirus factors involved in cholesterol homeostasis. This coronavirus essentiality catalog could inform ongoing drug development efforts aimed at intercepting and treating COVID-19, and help prepare for future coronavirus outbreaks.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-11-25T19:01:19.445Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/9rc2n4bkmv","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/9rc2n4bkmv"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"JT","last_name":"Poirier","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Coronavirus","Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus 2","COVID-19"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"st2xscfb4r","title":"Immune Imprinting and Antibody Profiles to SARS-CoV-2 in Urban and Rural Ghana","description":"Infections with SARS-CoV-2 and the development of immunity to the virus and its antigenic variants have been unevenly documented globally, with African populations particularly understudied. As SARS-CoV-2 transitions towards being an endemic pathogen, more nuanced understanding of immune protection in diverse populations is required. In 2023, we conducted a cross-sectional study of 1,000 Ghanaian residents to assess SARS-CoV-2 prevalence and antibody correlates of immunity against SARS-CoV-2 variants. We found an active SARS-CoV-2 infection rate of 1.3% and a 57% vaccination rate. We observed anti-SARS-CoV-2 Spike plasma antibody sero-positivity of 98.7%, with urban compared to rural residents having higher anti-SARS-CoV-2 plasma and saliva antibody concentrations. Vaccinated and urban individuals exhibited significantly greater Spike-pseudotyped virus neutralization than nonvaccinated and rural individuals. Notably, plasma antibodies preferentially bound Wuhan-Hu-1 SARS-CoV-2 over Omicron Spike variants. Our findings indicate significant prior and ongoing SARS-CoV-2 transmission as well as immunological imprinting by Wuhan-Hu-1-like SARS-CoV-2 in Ghana. \n\nAntigen and antibody data in this study were generated using electrochemiluminescence (ECL)-based Meso Scale Discovery (MSD) assays. This Mendeley dataset includes MSD Arbitrary Units derived from ECL data. Additionally, the dataset contains Spike-pseudotyped virus neutralization percentages and deidentified demographic information for the study participants, including town of residence, sex, and COVID-19 vaccination status. Age information has been excluded to maintain participant privacy.\n\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-04-25T09:53:33.161Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/st2xscfb4r","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/st2xscfb4r"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Martin","last_name":"Montiel-Ruiz","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Elvis S.","last_name":"Lomotey","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Elizabeth","last_name":"Obeng-Aboagye","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Isaac","last_name":"Quaye","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Daniel A. ","last_name":"Odumang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Florence B. ","last_name":"Amakye","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Bernard A.","last_name":"Logonia","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Salomé","last_name":"Lochmann","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Joseph A.","last_name":"Hayford","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Dickson K.","last_name":"Osabutey","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Angelica","last_name":"Daakyire","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Christopher","last_name":"Dorcoo","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Edward","last_name":"Dumashie","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Joseph","last_name":"Quartey","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Dorothy","last_name":"Yeboah-Manu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"George B. ","last_name":"Sigal","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Scott D. ","last_name":"Boyd","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Irene","last_name":"Owusu Donkor","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Katharina","last_name":"Röltgen","orcid":"0000-0002-1582-1720"}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/00mt8k932","name":"State Secretariat for Education, Research and Innovation","grant_id":null},{"id":"https://ror.org/00yjd3n13","name":"Swiss National Science Foundation","grant_id":"PR00P3_208580"}],"categories":["Viral Immunology","Mucosal Immunity","Electrochemiluminescence","Imprinting","Immunoglobulin G","Neutralization","Infectious Disease Serology","Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus 2","COVID-19"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"n2cyh3pk4h","title":"Attenuated replication and damaging effects of SARS-CoV-2 Omicron variants in an intestinal epithelial barrier model","description":"Many COVID-19 patients suffer from gastrointestinal symptoms and impaired intestinal barrier function may play a key role in harmful inflammatory responses and Long COVID. Despite its importance, the impact of SARS-CoV-2 on intestinal epithelia is poorly understood. To address this, we established an intestinal barrier model integrating epithelial Caco-2 cells and mucus-secreting HT29 cells in the presence or absence of human Raji cells. We show that this gut epithelial model allows efficient differentiation of Caco-2 cells into M (microfold) cell-like cells and faithfully mimics intestinal barrier function. It is highly permissive to SARS-CoV-2 infection due to high expression of the viral entry factors ACE2 and TMPRSS2. Early strains of SARS-CoV-2 and the Delta variant replicated with high efficiency, severely disrupted barrier function and depleted tight junction proteins, such as claudin-1, occludin and ZO-1. In comparison, Omicron subvariants also depleted ZO-1 from tight junctions but had less damaging effects on mucosal integrity and barrier function. Remdesivir and the TMPRSS2 inhibitor Camostat prevented SARS-CoV-2 replication and thus epithelial barrier damage, while the Cathepsin inhibitor E64d was ineffective. Our results support that SARS-CoV-2 disrupts intestinal barrier function but further suggest that circulating Omicron variants are less damaging than earlier viral strains.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-02-16T14:22:56.963Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/n2cyh3pk4h","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/n2cyh3pk4h"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Meta","last_name":"Volcic","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Frank","last_name":"Kirchhoff","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Rayhane","last_name":"Nchioua","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Chiara","last_name":"Pastorio","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Fabian","last_name":"Zech","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Clarissa","last_name":"Read","orcid":null},{"first_name":"paul","last_name":"walther","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","grant_id":"CRC1279"}],"categories":["Intestine","Tight Junction","Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus 2","Intestinal Mucosal Barrier"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"335br4c2xb","title":"SARS‑CoV‑2 complete genome sequencing from the Italian Campania region using a highly automated next generation sequencing system","description":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34090468/\n\nDOI: 10.1186/s12967-021-02912-4 \n\n\n\nBackground: Since the first complete genome sequencing of SARS-CoV-2 in December 2019, more than 550,000 genomes have been submitted into the GISAID database. Sequencing of the SARS-CoV-2 genome might allow identification of variants with increased contagiousness, different clinical patterns and/or different response to vaccines. A highly automated next generation sequencing (NGS)-based method might facilitate an active genomic surveillance of the virus.\n\nMethods: RNA was extracted from 27 nasopharyngeal swabs obtained from citizens of the Italian Campania region in March-April 2020 who tested positive for SARS-CoV-2. Following viral RNA quantification, sequencing was performed using the Ion AmpliSeq SARS-CoV-2 Research Panel on the Genexus Integrated Sequencer, an automated technology for library preparation and sequencing. The SARS-CoV-2 complete genomes were built using the pipeline SARS-CoV-2 RECoVERY (REconstruction of COronaVirus gEnomes & Rapid analYsis) and analysed by IQ-TREE software.\n\nResults: The complete genome (100%) of SARS-CoV-2 was successfully obtained for 21/27 samples. In particular, the complete genome was fully sequenced for all 15 samples with high viral titer (> 200 copies/µl), for the two samples with a viral genome copy number < 200 but greater than 20, and for 4/10 samples with a viral load < 20 viral copies. The complete genome sequences classified into the B.1 and B.1.1 SARS-CoV-2 lineages. In comparison to the reference strain Wuhan-Hu-1, 48 total nucleotide variants were observed with 26 non-synonymous substitutions, 18 synonymous and 4 reported in untranslated regions (UTRs). Ten of the 26 non-synonymous variants were observed in ORF1ab, 7 in S, 1 in ORF3a, 2 in M and 6 in N genes.\n\nConclusions: The Genexus system resulted successful for SARS-CoV-2 complete genome sequencing, also in cases with low viral copies. The use of this highly automated system might facilitate the standardization of SARS-CoV-2 sequencing protocols and make faster the identification of novel variants during the pandemic.\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-10-19T13:01:22.558Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/335br4c2xb","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/335br4c2xb"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Anna Maria","last_name":"Rachiglio","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Luca","last_name":"De Sabato","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Cristin ","last_name":"Roma","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Michele ","last_name":"Cennamo","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Mariano","last_name":"Fiorenza","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Daniela ","last_name":"Terracciano","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Raffaella ","last_name":"Pasquale","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Francesca ","last_name":"Bergantino","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ernesta ","last_name":"Cavalcanti","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Gerardo ","last_name":"Botti","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Gabriele","last_name":"Vaccari","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Giuseppe","last_name":"Portella","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Nicola ","last_name":"Normanno","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Next Generation Sequencing"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"hfbhym45fz","title":"CRISPR screening reveals a requirement for ribosome recycling in efficient SARS-CoV-2 programmed ribosomal frameshifting and viral replication. Rehfeld et al.","description":"Supplemental Table S1. Results of SARS-CoV-2 programmed ribosomal frameshifting genome-wide CRISPR screens (MAGeCK analysis).\nSupplemental Table S2. Oligonucleotides.\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-01-19T12:17:58.922Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/hfbhym45fz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/hfbhym45fz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Joshua","last_name":"Mendell","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Frederick","last_name":"Rehfeld","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/0003xa228","name":"Cancer Prevention and Research Institute of Texas","grant_id":"RP220309"},{"id":"https://ror.org/00np6vq88","name":"Welch Foundation","grant_id":"I-1961-20210327"},{"id":"https://ror.org/006w34k90","name":"Howard Hughes Medical Institute","grant_id":null}],"categories":["Oligonucleotide","CRISPR"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"rnsbwdr7gw","title":"Human Early Syncytiotrophoblasts Are Highly Susceptible to SARS-CoV-2 Infection. Ruan et al","description":"Direct in vivo investigation of human placenta trophoblast’s susceptibility to SARS-CoV-2 is challenging. Here we report that human trophoblast stem cells (hTSCs) and their derivatives are susceptible to SARS-CoV-2 infection which reveal heterogeneity in hTSC cultures. Early syncytiotrophoblasts (eSTBs) generated from hTSCs have enriched transcriptomic features of peri-implantation trophoblasts, express high levels of angiotensin-converting enzyme 2 (ACE2) and are productively infected by SARS-CoV-2 and its Delta and Omicron variants to produce virions. Antiviral drugs suppress SARS-CoV-2 replication in eSTBs and antagonize the virus-induced blockage of STB maturation. Though less susceptible to SARS-CoV-infection, viral replication in trophoblast organoids originated from hTSCs are detectable, reminiscent of the uncommon placenta infection. These findings implicate possible risks of COVID-19 infection in peri-implantation embryos which may go unnoticed. Stem cell-derived human trophoblasts such as eSTBs can potentially provide unlimited amounts of normal and genome-edited cells and facilitate coronavirus research and antiviral discovery. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-11-14T17:32:38.247Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/rnsbwdr7gw","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/rnsbwdr7gw"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Pentao","last_name":"Liu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Stem Cell","Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus 2"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":111826,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":141,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":111826,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":111468,"restricted":60,"embargoed":298},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":1841},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":578},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Centre","count":414},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":379},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":370},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":366},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":325},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":275},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":270},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":219}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":109333},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1851},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":176},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":107},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":65},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":64},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":48},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":31},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":31},{"id":"5f1edf21-7948-4d82-894f-c0ed4b1e8f52","name":"Teesside University","count":25}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":465},{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":402},{"id":"f58d7a83-6efa-5d8a-a6c2-b9e78ee14783","name":"Maharishi Markandeshwar Institute of Physiotherapy and Rehabilitation","count":357},{"id":"9d63ad1d-c391-5c35-a12e-cafe17017098","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid","count":299},{"id":"55ba66b2-f276-5204-a3d8-acfa499794e0","name":"The University of Manchester","count":291}]}}